`,s.jsx(R,{items:[{q:"What is SSD's core architectural difference from YOLO v1?",a:"Prediction happens on six feature maps of decreasing resolution rather than a single 7x7 map. Early high-resolution maps have small receptive fields and handle small objects; deep coarse maps handle large ones. It gets multi-scale detection from the network's own hierarchy, with no image pyramid and no extra forward passes."},{q:"Where do SSD300's 8,732 default boxes come from, and where are most of them?",a:"38²x4 + 19²x6 + 10²x6 + 5²x6 + 3²x4 + 1²x4 = 5,776 + 2,166 + 600 + 150 + 36 + 4. About 66% sit on conv4_3, the highest-resolution map — deliberately, because small objects are the hard case and need the fine grid."},{q:"Why does SSD need hard negative mining when Faster R-CNN's head does not?",a:"Because SSD computes a loss over every one of its 8,732 default boxes, of which roughly 0.1% are matched to an object, so the background term outweighs the positives by about a thousand to one. Faster R-CNN's second stage is trained on a sampled, roughly balanced minibatch of a few hundred RoIs, so the imbalance never reaches the loss. SSD sorts negatives by confidence loss and keeps only the worst, capped at 3 per positive."},{q:"Default boxes at one layer share a scale but vary in aspect ratio. What does that split buy?",a:"It separates two independent variables cleanly. The feature map's resolution and receptive field determine what size of object a cell can see, so scale is the map's job; the default boxes then only have to cover shape at that scale, using w = s*sqrt(a) and h = s/sqrt(a), which keeps the area equal to s² for every ratio. That way adding aspect ratios does not disturb the scale assignment."},{q:"SSD300 gets 74.3 mAP at 59 fps against Faster R-CNN's 73.2 at 5 fps. What is the general lesson?",a:"That cheap dense predictions on a shared feature hierarchy beat expensive per-region computation — provided you handle the class imbalance that cheapness creates. It is not 'more boxes is better'; the 8,732 boxes are nearly free precisely because they are read off feature maps that were computed anyway, and they are only usable because hard negative mining stops the background from swamping the loss."}]}),`
